NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"infinite-query-first-paint-oracle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Infinite-query first-paint oracle"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed semantic commit: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"415a8d4a1ee0b950eca6f06f25c250adab05a15d"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Comparison main: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"987f6ca0dfc404de8c76bd8c2b840421cc26311e"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Owner: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db/tests/conformance/infinite-suite-oracle.ts"},{"type":"text","value":", scenario "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[first-paint-ready]"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Supporting contract: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db/tests/conformance/infinite-contract.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"InfiniteQueryObservedHandle.firstPaint"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mountObserved"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Per-driver recorders: React "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/react-db/tests/infinite-query-conformance.test.tsx"},{"type":"text","value":", Vue "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/vue-db/tests/infinite-query-conformance.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":", Svelte "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/svelte-db/tests/infinite-query-conformance.svelte.test.ts"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Independent cross-hook reference: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/react-db/tests/infinite-query-first-commit-parity.test.tsx"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Runtime: local Node, Vitest. React ran in full. Vue and Svelte ran before the rebase and again under per-framework spot checks. CI runs every package suite."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"new-term","children":[{"type":"text","value":"New term"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This change introduces the observation term "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"first paint"}]},{"type":"text","value":". A first paint is\nthe first value a framework binding publishes to its consumer after mount. The\nterm is not in the project glossary yet. Each driver records the first paint\nthrough its own native commit hook, because the frameworks reach it through\ndifferent cuts:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React records every layout commit and keeps the first. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"current()"},{"type":"text","value":" cannot\nserve the law, because React can coalesce the idle commit before "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"renderHook"},{"type":"text","value":"\nreturns."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vue reads the hook result immediately after setup. Its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"watchEffect"},{"type":"text","value":" with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"flush: 'sync'"},{"type":"text","value":" already subscribed and produced the first value."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Svelte reads the construction snapshot before "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"flushSync"},{"type":"text","value":", which is its first\nrender value, ahead of the subscribing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"$effect"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The term describes a cross-framework observation. It does not combine or split a\nproduction concept."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"requirement-outcomes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirement outcomes"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Result"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The law: for a synchronously loaded source, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveInfiniteQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" shows the ready first page on the first paint, with no empty idle paint before data. Limit: synchronous eager sources and the query-callback input form. The recorder observes a framework commit value, not a browser paint."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The expected first page "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"['1','2','3']"},{"type":"text","value":" comes from the source rows and the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"orderBy(rank desc)"},{"type":"text","value":" plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pageSize"},{"type":"text","value":" arguments, independent of the hook output. The parity test adds "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" as a documented prior-behavior reference. Neither reads the controller snapshot logic to compute the expectation."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The scenario prose states the law and why "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"current()"},{"type":"text","value":" cannot observe it. The contract comment states the first-paint obligation and the per-framework cut. The driver records the real hook; the checkpoint reads "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"firstPaint()"},{"type":"text","value":" after flush."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. The trigger is a generated property that claims history coverage. This is a fixed example scenario over one synchronous source. It makes no generated-history coverage claim."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Each driver mounts the real framework hook and records the real first paint through the framework commit hook, then flushes to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ready"},{"type":"text","value":". The checkpoint is the first recorded paint."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Mutant calibration: reverting React or Svelte to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"startSync: false"},{"type":"text","value":" makes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[first-paint-ready]"},{"type":"text","value":" fail at the first-paint checkpoint with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ status: 'idle', ids: [] }"},{"type":"text","value":". This is an assertion failure at the intended checkpoint, not a setup error. The fixed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"startSync: true"},{"type":"text","value":" passes."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. The trigger is an important generated property. This scenario has no random campaign. Direct replay is running the named "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[first-paint-ready]"},{"type":"text","value":" scenario in each driver package."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. The change adds no production state and no stateful model. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"firstPaint()"},{"type":"text","value":" is a single test-only observation; it does not introduce, remove, combine, or split a modeled state."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The one new term is \"first paint\", declared above with its per-framework mapping. No production concept is combined or split."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The React recorder keeps the first observed commit and throws when no commit was observed, so it cannot silently report an empty paint. Cleanup uses the suite's existing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"track()"},{"type":"text","value":" and lifetime teardown; it does not replace the assertion."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The parity test is an independent second formulation: it asserts that "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveInfiniteQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" agree on the first commit for the same synchronous source. No further distinct semantic classifier was identified."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. This versioned record accounts for ORC-001 through ORC-014 at the reviewed semantic commit above. The pull-request description alone did not satisfy this requirement."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-013"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The scenario protects a reusable cross-framework boundary law. The distinguishing witness is the mutant: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"startSync: false"},{"type":"text","value":" fails for React and Svelte at the first-paint checkpoint, while the fix passes. Vue's distinct sync-subscribe cut stays green under both, which the law allows."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-014"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. No controlled provider or host supplies a premise. The source is a standard synchronous mock collection shared with the rest of the suite."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"bug-class-closure","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bug-class closure"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The claim is bounded, not universal."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Boundary. Contract: ready first page on the first paint for a synchronous\nsource. History: mount of a query-form "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveInfiniteQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" over a\nsynchronous eager source, plus React dependency replacement. Production path:\nthe React, Vue, and Svelte hooks that build the live-query window collection.\nObservation: the first paint status and first-page ids."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Distinguishing witnesses. Original: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"startSync: false"},{"type":"text","value":" yields an idle first\npaint for React and Svelte. Adjacent: Vue's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"watchEffect({ flush: 'sync' })"},{"type":"text","value":"\nyields a ready first paint even with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"startSync: false"},{"type":"text","value":", which shows the law\nconstrains the observable first paint, not the flag."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rejected wrong design. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"startSync: false"},{"type":"text","value":", the production defect, is rejected\nat the first-paint checkpoint for React and Svelte."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unresolved in-scope cells remain open under the owner above:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pre-created collection input form, rather than the query callback."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Suspense, concurrent, or StrictMode first paint."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Out-of-scope cells, where an empty or loading first paint is correct:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Asynchronous or on-demand sources that are not yet loaded."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dbClient"},{"type":"text","value":"-materialized sources whose sync is deferred to commit."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"verification","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Verification"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"react-db: the full suite passed locally (330 tests), including this scenario\nand the mutant run."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vue and Svelte: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[first-paint-ready]"},{"type":"text","value":" and the package suites passed under spot\nchecks. I could not re-run them against the latest "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":" locally, because the\nsandbox npm proxy returned 403 for unrelated security-bumped dependencies. CI\nruns every package suite."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This record does not mark any CI check green."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"addendum-duplicate-pre-commit-render-fix","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Addendum: duplicate pre-commit render fix"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A follow-up review found a regression from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"startSync: true"},{"type":"text","value":": because the hooks\nbuilt a new collection per render and only recorded it at commit, a duplicate\npre-commit render (React StrictMode, a discarded concurrent render, or a\nSuspense retry) started a second collection and loaded an on-demand source's\nfirst page twice. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" avoids this with a render-time instance memo\n(its pool excludes window and on-demand queries, so the pool is not the cause)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fix: React now records each render's state in a render-time ref and reuses it\nwhen every identity input matches, while committed state is still recorded at\nsubscribe so an abandoned render cannot overwrite preserved pages. Svelte's\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"$derived"},{"type":"text","value":" controller now reuses the previous controller when nothing that\ndefines it changed, instead of rebuilding on every recompute."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Regression: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/react-db/tests/infinite-query-strictmode-dedup.test.tsx"},{"type":"text","value":"\nmounts an on-demand source under StrictMode and asserts the first peek-ahead\nwindow loads once. It fails (two loads) when the memo is recorded only at\ncommit, and passes with the render-time reuse. This closes the StrictMode part\nof the previously unresolved cell. The pre-created-collection input form and\nSuspense or concurrent first paint remain open under the owner above."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A later review found that the React reuse could bind a collection that GC had\nalready cleaned up. A committed component keeps its refs across a suspended\nupdate, so the update's uncommitted collection survived to the retry, and the\nretry committed an empty "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleaned-up"},{"type":"text","value":" page before recovering. The reuse now\nrequires a collection that is not "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleaned-up"},{"type":"text","value":".\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/react-db/tests/infinite-query-stale-reuse.test.tsx"},{"type":"text","value":" covers both a\nfresh-mount retry and a mounted suspended update after GC. The mounted-update\ncase fails without the liveness check and passes with it."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"addendum-start-sync-gate-replaces-render-time-reuse","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Addendum: start-sync gate replaces render-time reuse"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A further review found that the two reuse mechanisms above caused new\ndefects. In Svelte, the reuse guard returned the previous controller when the\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deps"},{"type":"text","value":" getters were unchanged, so the hook ignored reactive state read directly\ninside the query callback. In React, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"renderedRef"},{"type":"text","value":" could keep a stale page\ncount. It did not dedupe React 18 StrictMode renders, and it duplicated the\nidentity rules."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both mechanisms are removed. The duplicate page requests came only from\non-demand sources, which load asynchronously and gain nothing from an early\nstart. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"canStartLiveQueryWindowSyncInRender"},{"type":"text","value":" now starts sync during render only\nwhen no source collection is on-demand. Eager sources keep the ready first\npaint. On-demand queries start when the subscription commits."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Evidence at this change:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"infinite-query-strictmode-dedup.test.tsx"},{"type":"text","value":" passes with one first-page load.\nIt fails with two loads when the gate always starts sync."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveInfiniteQuery.svelte.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" adds a test where state read inside the\nquery callback, with no deps, rebuilds the query. It fails with the removed\nSvelte guard and passes now."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"infinite-query-stale-reuse.test.tsx"},{"type":"text","value":" passes. A retry after GC binds a live\ncollection and shows the ready first page."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Local suites passed: react-db 333, vue-db 121, svelte-db 118, and the db\ninfinite calibration 9."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Known limit: an eager collection started during render has only the 50 ms\nunsubscribed GC floor before its commit. If a commit arrives later than that,\nGC may clean the collection up first. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" shares this exposure. No\ntest reaches this case."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"addendum-nested-on-demand-sources-and-retained-page-windows","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Addendum: nested on-demand sources and retained-page windows"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A review of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"725e37a28ac81e3d5041d990eb4abeebedc60399"},{"type":"text","value":" reproduced two\nregressions from starting sync in render:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The start gate read only a query's immediate sources. A live-query\nCollection does not copy its sources' sync mode, so an on-demand source\nbehind one passed the gate. An abandoned React Suspense render then sent a\npage request. The gate now follows each live-query Collection to the sources\nit reads."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A dependency replacement that keeps page depth built its collection with a\none-page window and started it in render. React committed a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ready"},{"type":"text","value":" result\nwith fewer rows than the retained pages and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hasNextPage: false"},{"type":"text","value":", then\ncorrected it. React and Svelte now size the new window for every retained\npage."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Evidence, produced on the working tree above that commit:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/react-db/tests/infinite-query-render-start.test.tsx"},{"type":"text","value":" has three\ntests. A wrapped on-demand source gets no acquisition from an abandoned\nrender, and a committed control acquires it. Every ready commit after an\nequal dependency replacement keeps all retained rows and continuation."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mutants: without the nested walk, only the abandoned-render test fails. With\na one-page window, only the retained-pages test fails."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveInfiniteQuery.svelte.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" checks that no on-demand source, direct\nor wrapped, is acquired at construction or by a superseded recompute before\nthe subscribing effect runs, and that one is acquired after it runs. Against\na "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"db"},{"type":"text","value":" build without the nested walk, the wrapped case loaded once at\nconstruction."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Local suites: react-db 336, vue-db 121, svelte-db 119, and the db infinite\ncalibration 9."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Svelte's retained-page window was fixed by the same rule, but no test observes\nan intermediate Svelte value. The coverage map lists that cell."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"addendum-publication-laws-in-the-shared-oracle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Addendum: publication laws in the shared oracle"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This entry moves the laws found in the earlier reviews into the shared\ninfinite-query suite where they are framework-independent, and leaves only\npre-commit work to the drivers."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Law"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Owner"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A synchronous source's first published value is ready with the first page."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Shared "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"first-paint-ready"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every ready value in a fixed-source, fixed-query scenario equals the source prefix for its own page count, with matching pages and continuation."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Shared, in 11 scenarios including "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"equal-dependency-depth"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"circular-dependency"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A mount requests an on-demand first window once."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Shared "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on-demand-paging"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A render that never commits, or a superseded pre-commit recompute, does not acquire an on-demand source, direct or wrapped; commit does. Retired by the addendum \"On-demand sources follow useLiveQuery\"; replaced by acquisition parity with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"infinite-query-render-cuts-oracle.test.tsx"},{"type":"text","value":", Svelte hook tests"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A StrictMode double render requests an on-demand first window once. Narrowed by the same addendum to React 19, where both hooks keep refs across the double render."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React render cuts"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"After GC reclaims an abandoned render's collection, the retry's first commit is the ready first page."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React render cuts"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each shared handle now records every value its framework published, which\nreplaces the single first-paint recorder. Pre-commit work stays in drivers,\nbecause Vue's setup is its commit."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Evidence, produced on the working tree above "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0f0e05cbb"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With a one-page replacement window, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"equal-dependency-depth"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"circular-dependency"},{"type":"text","value":" fail in React and in Svelte. The Svelte half of the\nretained-page regression was real; no earlier test could observe it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"startSync: false"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"first-paint-ready"},{"type":"text","value":" fails in React and Svelte."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In the React render-cuts file, removing the nested walk fails only the\nwrapped abandoned-render test. Starting every source in render also fails the\nStrictMode test."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The four React-only files from earlier entries are merged into one. Their\nretained-page test is dropped, because the shared law covers it in every\ndriver."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Local suites: react-db 335, vue-db 121, svelte-db 119, and the db infinite\ncalibration 9."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A probe found one more open cell. A supplied collection that has not started\npublishes an idle first value in React and Svelte, and a ready one in Vue.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" starts a supplied collection in render. The infinite hook does\nnot, and a React test requires that an abandoned render leave a supplied\ncollection unsubscribed. Closing this cell changes that contract, so it is\nrecorded for decision rather than changed here."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"addendum-supplied-collections-match-uselivequery","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Addendum: supplied collections match useLiveQuery"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Decision: the maintainer chose to make "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveInfiniteQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" behave like\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" for a supplied collection, and to reverse the rule from #1675\nthat no supplied collection starts before commit. That rule came from a #1675\nreview, which asked that collection construction be inert so that a render\nthat never commits leaves no resources behind. This PR keeps the purpose of\nthat rule with two safeguards: GC reclaims an abandoned start, and on-demand\nsources still wait for commit. The contract test\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"does not activate a supplied collection for an abandoned render"},{"type":"text","value":" is replaced\nby "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reclaims a supplied collection that an abandoned render started"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A supplied collection starts during render only when no source behind it is\non-demand and its window already holds the requested rows from offset 0. A\nshifted or narrower window waits for the controller to adjust it at commit.\nBoth hooks now validate a query or collection before starting it, so a\nrejected "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".findOne()"},{"type":"text","value":" query never reads its source."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Evidence, produced on the working tree above "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"083247d18"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The shared "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"first-paint-ready-collection"},{"type":"text","value":" scenario failed in React and\nSvelte with an idle first value, and passed in Vue, before the change. All\nthree drivers pass after it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection-window-normalization"},{"type":"text","value":" now checks every ready value. A supplied\ngate that ignores the window fails it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A supplied gate that ignores on-demand sources fails the supplied-input case\nof the React render-cuts abandoned-render test."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"findone-runtime"},{"type":"text","value":" now requires an unread source. Starting before validation\nfails it with two subscribers."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"live-query-window-controller.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" adds a controlled-premise witness for\na commit that arrives after the 50 ms GC floor. The collection is cleaned up\nfirst, and subscribing restarts it without publishing a non-ready value. A\nrestart guard that skips cleaned-up collections fails it. Delivering the\nsubscribe handshake's publications to the listener does not. The witness\ndrives the controller directly, because a test DOM cannot hold a React\ncommit, so it does not prove React scheduling."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The walk from a live-query Collection to its sources is one shared helper,\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"everySourceCollection"},{"type":"text","value":", which the observer's persisted-readiness check also\nuses. The addendum \"Supplied windows request only what the hook needs\"\nremoves it with the on-demand gate it served."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Local suites: react-db 338, vue-db 122, svelte-db 120, and the full db suite\nof 8267 tests."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Still open: an on-demand source that loads synchronously publishes ready\nfirst in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" and idle first in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveInfiniteQuery"},{"type":"text","value":", because the\ninfinite hook defers on-demand sources to commit. The two hooks differ there\nuntil that choice is made. With a DbClient, a source that is first created\nduring render waits for commit in both hooks, by the deferral contract from\n#1564."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"addendum-on-demand-sources-follow-uselivequery","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Addendum: on-demand sources follow useLiveQuery"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Decision: the maintainer chose that "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveInfiniteQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" follow\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" for on-demand sources too, unless that is plainly wrong. The\ntwo rules conflict for an on-demand source. Ready on the first paint needs a\nrequest during render, and no request before commit forbids one.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" starts on-demand sources in render, so the infinite hook now\ndoes too. The start gate is removed, and the law that a render that never\ncommits sends no on-demand request is retired. A parity law replaces it: under\nthe same history, the infinite hook acquires an on-demand source as often as\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" does, and commits the same first value."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two parts of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" were matched in substance, not copied:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" dedupes a React 19 StrictMode double render with a\nrender-time instance memo. The infinite hook now keeps the window collection\nfrom its latest render and reuses it when the query identity matches, the\ncollection is not "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleaned-up"},{"type":"text","value":", and its window holds the retained pages. It\nkeeps only the collection. Each render still builds its own controller and\npage count from committed state. One identity rule serves both the committed\nand the rendered baseline."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A supplied collection starts in render as in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":", but only when\nits window already holds the requested rows from offset 0. Starting a\nshifted or narrower window would publish the wrong rows first, which breaks\nthe ready-value law."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Evidence, produced on the working tree above "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"e6a31f6eb"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Before the change, an on-demand source that loads synchronously committed\nready first in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" and idle first in the infinite hook. With the\ngate removed alone, a StrictMode mount sent two first-window requests in the\ninfinite hook and one in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":". With the memo, both send one, and\nboth send the same count for an abandoned render."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"infinite-query-render-cuts-oracle.test.tsx"},{"type":"text","value":" compares both hooks for an\nabandoned render with a direct source, a wrapped source, and a supplied\ncollection, and for a StrictMode mount. Deferring query collections to\ncommit fails the abandoned-render and StrictMode cases. Deferring supplied\ncollections fails the supplied case. Removing the memo fails the StrictMode\ncase. Removing the memo's liveness check fails the mounted suspended-update\nretry with a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleaned-up"},{"type":"text","value":" commit."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveInfiniteQuery.svelte.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" compares settled acquisition counts\nwith Svelte's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" after construction and a superseded recompute,\nfor direct and wrapped sources. Deferring query collections fails it and\nSvelte "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"first-paint-ready"},{"type":"text","value":". The two hooks issue their requests at different\nmoments inside the same tick; only the settled count is compared."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Local suites: react-db 337, vue-db 122, svelte-db 120, and the full db suite\nof 8267 tests."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Remaining exposure, shared with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" by this decision: a render that\nnever commits, including a Suspense render that throws, sends on-demand\nrequests. On React 18, StrictMode does not keep refs across the double render,\nso both hooks send two first-window requests there. Removing early on-demand\nrequests from both hooks needs a change in the live-query Collection itself."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"addendum-supplied-windows-request-only-what-the-hook-needs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Addendum: supplied windows request only what the hook needs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An external review of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"c3341af0f"},{"type":"text","value":" raised ten findings. This entry records the\nbehavioral ones."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Law: a mount requests no on-demand rows beyond the window the hook needs, for\neither input form. Authority: established behavior on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":", where a supplied\ncollection waits for the controller to set its window at commit. The shared\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on-demand-paging"},{"type":"text","value":" scenario covered only query callbacks, and the supplied\nscenarios used an exact "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".limit(4)"},{"type":"text","value":" window, so a wider window was never\ngenerated. The new shared scenario "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on-demand-collection-window"},{"type":"text","value":" declares a\nwider "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".limit(10)"},{"type":"text","value":" and an exact "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".limit(4)"},{"type":"text","value":", and checks the rows the\non-demand source holds after mount, which are the rows it was asked for."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With the earlier rule, which started any window at least as wide as needed,\nthe scenario fails in React and Svelte: the source loads all eight rows. It\npasses on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":" and with the repair. Vue starts at commit and passes both."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The repair starts a supplied collection in render only when its window is\nexactly the needed one, or unbounded. An unbounded window, from a query with\nno "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".limit"},{"type":"text","value":", sends one unbounded request on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":" too, because the\ncontroller cannot narrow it before the source request, so starting it in\nrender adds no request and keeps its first paint ready. That pre-existing\nunbounded request is outside this law and stays open."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The React render-time reuse path uses the same rule. A reuse mutant that\naccepts a wider window survives every suite: the reused collection's rows\nare already loaded, so no observation distinguishes it. No harm was found."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In Svelte, starting sync inside "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"$derived.by"},{"type":"text","value":" can throw\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state_unsafe_mutation"},{"type":"text","value":" when the start synchronously writes rows a peer's\npending load is waiting for. A two-component probe reproduces it for the\ninfinite hook and, on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":", for Svelte's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":", which starts in a\nderived too. On the follow-up branch, where a live-query Collection defers\nacquisition until a subscriber or preload, the same probe passes for both\nhooks.\nDecision: the maintainer accepted this exposure in this PR, shared with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":", and the deferred-acquisition follow-up removes it for both."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On React 18, StrictMode does not keep refs across the double render, so both\nhooks still send two first-window requests. The changeset now names React 19."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"addendum-render-time-reuse-follows-uselivequery-s-identity-and-error-laws","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Addendum: render-time reuse follows useLiveQuery's identity and error laws"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A second external review of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"16a34e061"},{"type":"text","value":" reproduced two React regressions in\nthe render-time collection cache, both against "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" as the\nreference."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A mounted replacement whose synchronous startup throws reached the error\nboundary under "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" but committed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status: error"},{"type":"text","value":" under the\ninfinite hook. React retried the render, and the retry reused the collection\nthe failed render had cached before it started. The hook now caches a created\ncollection only after it validated and started. The render-cut oracle,\nrenamed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"infinite-query-render-cuts-oracle.test.tsx"},{"type":"text","value":", compares both hooks on\nthis history; caching before startup fails the infinite case."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A mounted hook rerendered with a different source object under a claimed\nCollection ID showed the old source's rows, after a suspended update and,\nbefore this PR, after a committed one too. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" rejects that\nhistory. Its rule now lives in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"source-id-bindings.ts"},{"type":"text","value":", shared by both hooks,\nand the source ID reuse oracle adds an infinite-hook driver over its model:\ndirect reuse, reuse after another ID, the same object and a new ID, reuse\nafter a suspended render, and release of a shared descriptor's sync deferral\non rejection. Removing the infinite hook's claim fails five of them, and a\nclaim that releases nothing fails the deferral case."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on-demand-paging"},{"type":"text","value":" and the exact supplied window now assert the first value\nitself, not only later ready values, and the StrictMode parity test asserts\none first-window request on React 19. An infinite hook that leaves query\ncollections unstarted in render fails "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on-demand-paging"},{"type":"text","value":", and one without\nrender-time reuse fails the StrictMode test."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A third review of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"f490f3d4d"},{"type":"text","value":" found that the cache also reused a collection\nthat entered terminal error after it was cached, for example when its source\nrestarted after cleanup, so selecting its query again showed stale rows with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status: error"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" builds a fresh collection there. The cache now\nrejects a collection in terminal error. The render-cut oracle compares both\nhooks on that history and checks the restarted source's rows; dropping the\nerror check fails the infinite case. A synchronous load failure still throws\nfrom render in both hooks, and an asynchronous one settles after commit, so\nneither path reaches the cache."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The deferred-acquisition follow-up must revisit the new first-value assertion\nfor "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on-demand-paging"},{"type":"text","value":": before a subscriber, its ordered on-demand window is\nunpublished, so that law and this one conflict and need a decision there."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"revision-index","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Revision index"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each entry's evidence applies to the revision named here. The first section's\ntable and closure apply to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"415a8d4a1"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Entry"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Revision"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Record and requirement outcomes"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"415a8d4a1"},{"type":"text","value":" (record added in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1c74a335d"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Duplicate pre-commit render fix"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"a4af6a251"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cleaned-up reuse liveness check"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fc5f136a5"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start-sync gate replaces render-time reuse"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"b31b84ff9"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nested on-demand sources and retained-page windows"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"2b461f8cd"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Publication laws in the shared oracle"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"083247d18"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Supplied collections match useLiveQuery"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"e6a31f6eb"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On-demand sources follow useLiveQuery"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"c3341af0f"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Supplied windows request only what the hook needs"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ff7549054"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Render-time reuse follows useLiveQuery's identity and error laws"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the commit that adds this entry, on top of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"16a34e061"}]}]]}],"headings":[{"id":"infinite-query-first-paint-oracle","text":"Infinite-query first-paint oracle","level":1},{"id":"new-term","text":"New term","level":2},{"id":"requirement-outcomes","text":"Requirement outcomes","level":2},{"id":"bug-class-closure","text":"Bug-class closure","level":2},{"id":"verification","text":"Verification","level":2},{"id":"addendum-duplicate-pre-commit-render-fix","text":"Addendum: duplicate pre-commit render fix","level":2},{"id":"addendum-start-sync-gate-replaces-render-time-reuse","text":"Addendum: start-sync gate replaces render-time reuse","level":2},{"id":"addendum-nested-on-demand-sources-and-retained-page-windows","text":"Addendum: nested on-demand sources and retained-page windows","level":2},{"id":"addendum-publication-laws-in-the-shared-oracle","text":"Addendum: publication laws in the shared oracle","level":2},{"id":"addendum-supplied-collections-match-uselivequery","text":"Addendum: supplied collections match useLiveQuery","level":2},{"id":"addendum-on-demand-sources-follow-uselivequery","text":"Addendum: on-demand sources follow useLiveQuery","level":2},{"id":"addendum-supplied-windows-request-only-what-the-hook-needs","text":"Addendum: supplied windows request only what the hook needs","level":2},{"id":"addendum-render-time-reuse-follows-uselivequery-s-identity-and-error-laws","text":"Addendum: render-time reuse follows useLiveQuery's identity and error laws","level":2},{"id":"revision-index","text":"Revision index","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# Infinite-query first-paint oracle\n\n- Reviewed semantic commit: `415a8d4a1ee0b950eca6f06f25c250adab05a15d`\n- Comparison main: `987f6ca0dfc404de8c76bd8c2b840421cc26311e`\n- Owner: `packages/db/tests/conformance/infinite-suite-oracle.ts`, scenario `[first-paint-ready]`\n- Supporting contract: `packages/db/tests/conformance/infinite-contract.ts` (`InfiniteQueryObservedHandle.firstPaint`, `mountObserved`)\n- Per-driver recorders: React `packages/react-db/tests/infinite-query-conformance.test.tsx`, Vue `packages/vue-db/tests/infinite-query-conformance.test.ts`, Svelte `packages/svelte-db/tests/infinite-query-conformance.svelte.test.ts`\n- Independent cross-hook reference: `packages/react-db/tests/infinite-query-first-commit-parity.test.tsx`\n- Runtime: local Node, Vitest. React ran in full. Vue and Svelte ran before the rebase and again under per-framework spot checks. CI runs every package suite.\n\n## New term\n\nThis change introduces the observation term **first paint**. A first paint is\nthe first value a framework binding publishes to its consumer after mount. The\nterm is not in the project glossary yet. Each driver records the first paint\nthrough its own native commit hook, because the frameworks reach it through\ndifferent cuts:\n\n- React records every layout commit and keeps the first. `current()` cannot\n  serve the law, because React can coalesce the idle commit before `renderHook`\n  returns.\n- Vue reads the hook result immediately after setup. Its `watchEffect` with\n  `flush: 'sync'` already subscribed and produced the first value.\n- Svelte reads the construction snapshot before `flushSync`, which is its first\n  render value, ahead of the subscribing `$effect`.\n\nThe term describes a cross-framework observation. It does not combine or split a\nproduction concept.\n\n## Requirement outcomes\n\n| Requirement | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 | Pass. The law: for a synchronously loaded source, `useLiveInfiniteQuery` shows the ready first page on the first paint, with no empty idle paint before data. Limit: synchronous eager sources and the query-callback input form. The recorder observes a framework commit value, not a browser paint. |\n| ORC-002 | Pass. The expected first page `['1','2','3']` comes from the source rows and the `orderBy(rank desc)` plus `pageSize` arguments, independent of the hook output. The parity test adds `useLiveQuery` as a documented prior-behavior reference. Neither reads the controller snapshot logic to compute the expectation. |\n| ORC-003 | Pass. The scenario prose states the law and why `current()` cannot observe it. The contract comment states the first-paint obligation and the per-framework cut. The driver records the real hook; the checkpoint reads `firstPaint()` after flush. |\n| ORC-004 | Not applicable. The trigger is a generated property that claims history coverage. This is a fixed example scenario over one synchronous source. It makes no generated-history coverage claim. |\n| ORC-005 | Pass. Each driver mounts the real framework hook and records the real first paint through the framework commit hook, then flushes to `ready`. The checkpoint is the first recorded paint. |\n| ORC-006 | Pass. Mutant calibration: reverting React or Svelte to `startSync: false` makes `[first-paint-ready]` fail at the first-paint checkpoint with `{ status: 'idle', ids: [] }`. This is an assertion failure at the intended checkpoint, not a setup error. The fixed `startSync: true` passes. |\n| ORC-007 | Not applicable. The trigger is an important generated property. This scenario has no random campaign. Direct replay is running the named `[first-paint-ready]` scenario in each driver package. |\n| ORC-008 | Not applicable. The change adds no production state and no stateful model. `firstPaint()` is a single test-only observation; it does not introduce, remove, combine, or split a modeled state. |\n| ORC-009 | Pass. The one new term is \"first paint\", declared above with its per-framework mapping. No production concept is combined or split. |\n| ORC-010 | Pass. The React recorder keeps the first observed commit and throws when no commit was observed, so it cannot silently report an empty paint. Cleanup uses the suite's existing `track()` and lifetime teardown; it does not replace the assertion. |\n| ORC-011 | Pass. The parity test is an independent second formulation: it asserts that `useLiveQuery` and `useLiveInfiniteQuery` agree on the first commit for the same synchronous source. No further distinct semantic classifier was identified. |\n| ORC-012 | Pass. This versioned record accounts for ORC-001 through ORC-014 at the reviewed semantic commit above. The pull-request description alone did not satisfy this requirement. |\n| ORC-013 | Pass. The scenario protects a reusable cross-framework boundary law. The distinguishing witness is the mutant: `startSync: false` fails for React and Svelte at the first-paint checkpoint, while the fix passes. Vue's distinct sync-subscribe cut stays green under both, which the law allows. |\n| ORC-014 | Not applicable. No controlled provider or host supplies a premise. The source is a standard synchronous mock collection shared with the rest of the suite. |\n\n## Bug-class closure\n\nThe claim is bounded, not universal.\n\n- Boundary. Contract: ready first page on the first paint for a synchronous\n  source. History: mount of a query-form `useLiveInfiniteQuery` over a\n  synchronous eager source, plus React dependency replacement. Production path:\n  the React, Vue, and Svelte hooks that build the live-query window collection.\n  Observation: the first paint status and first-page ids.\n- Distinguishing witnesses. Original: `startSync: false` yields an idle first\n  paint for React and Svelte. Adjacent: Vue's `watchEffect({ flush: 'sync' })`\n  yields a ready first paint even with `startSync: false`, which shows the law\n  constrains the observable first paint, not the flag.\n- Rejected wrong design. `startSync: false`, the production defect, is rejected\n  at the first-paint checkpoint for React and Svelte.\n\nUnresolved in-scope cells remain open under the owner above:\n\n- Pre-created collection input form, rather than the query callback.\n- Suspense, concurrent, or StrictMode first paint.\n\nOut-of-scope cells, where an empty or loading first paint is correct:\n\n- Asynchronous or on-demand sources that are not yet loaded.\n- `dbClient`-materialized sources whose sync is deferred to commit.\n\n## Verification\n\n- react-db: the full suite passed locally (330 tests), including this scenario\n  and the mutant run.\n- Vue and Svelte: `[first-paint-ready]` and the package suites passed under spot\n  checks. I could not re-run them against the latest `main` locally, because the\n  sandbox npm proxy returned 403 for unrelated security-bumped dependencies. CI\n  runs every package suite.\n- This record does not mark any CI check green.\n\n## Addendum: duplicate pre-commit render fix\n\nA follow-up review found a regression from `startSync: true`: because the hooks\nbuilt a new collection per render and only recorded it at commit, a duplicate\npre-commit render (React StrictMode, a discarded concurrent render, or a\nSuspense retry) started a second collection and loaded an on-demand source's\nfirst page twice. `useLiveQuery` avoids this with a render-time instance memo\n(its pool excludes window and on-demand queries, so the pool is not the cause).\n\nFix: React now records each render's state in a render-time ref and reuses it\nwhen every identity input matches, while committed state is still recorded at\nsubscribe so an abandoned render cannot overwrite preserved pages. Svelte's\n`$derived` controller now reuses the previous controller when nothing that\ndefines it changed, instead of rebuilding on every recompute.\n\nRegression: `packages/react-db/tests/infinite-query-strictmode-dedup.test.tsx`\nmounts an on-demand source under StrictMode and asserts the first peek-ahead\nwindow loads once. It fails (two loads) when the memo is recorded only at\ncommit, and passes with the render-time reuse. This closes the StrictMode part\nof the previously unresolved cell. The pre-created-collection input form and\nSuspense or concurrent first paint remain open under the owner above.\n\nA later review found that the React reuse could bind a collection that GC had\nalready cleaned up. A committed component keeps its refs across a suspended\nupdate, so the update's uncommitted collection survived to the retry, and the\nretry committed an empty `cleaned-up` page before recovering. The reuse now\nrequires a collection that is not `cleaned-up`.\n`packages/react-db/tests/infinite-query-stale-reuse.test.tsx` covers both a\nfresh-mount retry and a mounted suspended update after GC. The mounted-update\ncase fails without the liveness check and passes with it.\n\n## Addendum: start-sync gate replaces render-time reuse\n\nA further review found that the two reuse mechanisms above caused new\ndefects. In Svelte, the reuse guard returned the previous controller when the\n`deps` getters were unchanged, so the hook ignored reactive state read directly\ninside the query callback. In React, `renderedRef` could keep a stale page\ncount. It did not dedupe React 18 StrictMode renders, and it duplicated the\nidentity rules.\n\nBoth mechanisms are removed. The duplicate page requests came only from\non-demand sources, which load asynchronously and gain nothing from an early\nstart. `canStartLiveQueryWindowSyncInRender` now starts sync during render only\nwhen no source collection is on-demand. Eager sources keep the ready first\npaint. On-demand queries start when the subscription commits.\n\nEvidence at this change:\n\n- `infinite-query-strictmode-dedup.test.tsx` passes with one first-page load.\n  It fails with two loads when the gate always starts sync.\n- `useLiveInfiniteQuery.svelte.test.ts` adds a test where state read inside the\n  query callback, with no deps, rebuilds the query. It fails with the removed\n  Svelte guard and passes now.\n- `infinite-query-stale-reuse.test.tsx` passes. A retry after GC binds a live\n  collection and shows the ready first page.\n- Local suites passed: react-db 333, vue-db 121, svelte-db 118, and the db\n  infinite calibration 9.\n\nKnown limit: an eager collection started during render has only the 50 ms\nunsubscribed GC floor before its commit. If a commit arrives later than that,\nGC may clean the collection up first. `useLiveQuery` shares this exposure. No\ntest reaches this case.\n\n## Addendum: nested on-demand sources and retained-page windows\n\nA review of `725e37a28ac81e3d5041d990eb4abeebedc60399` reproduced two\nregressions from starting sync in render:\n\n- The start gate read only a query's immediate sources. A live-query\n  Collection does not copy its sources' sync mode, so an on-demand source\n  behind one passed the gate. An abandoned React Suspense render then sent a\n  page request. The gate now follows each live-query Collection to the sources\n  it reads.\n- A dependency replacement that keeps page depth built its collection with a\n  one-page window and started it in render. React committed a `ready` result\n  with fewer rows than the retained pages and `hasNextPage: false`, then\n  corrected it. React and Svelte now size the new window for every retained\n  page.\n\nEvidence, produced on the working tree above that commit:\n\n- `packages/react-db/tests/infinite-query-render-start.test.tsx` has three\n  tests. A wrapped on-demand source gets no acquisition from an abandoned\n  render, and a committed control acquires it. Every ready commit after an\n  equal dependency replacement keeps all retained rows and continuation.\n- Mutants: without the nested walk, only the abandoned-render test fails. With\n  a one-page window, only the retained-pages test fails.\n- `useLiveInfiniteQuery.svelte.test.ts` checks that no on-demand source, direct\n  or wrapped, is acquired at construction or by a superseded recompute before\n  the subscribing effect runs, and that one is acquired after it runs. Against\n  a `db` build without the nested walk, the wrapped case loaded once at\n  construction.\n- Local suites: react-db 336, vue-db 121, svelte-db 119, and the db infinite\n  calibration 9.\n\nSvelte's retained-page window was fixed by the same rule, but no test observes\nan intermediate Svelte value. The coverage map lists that cell.\n\n## Addendum: publication laws in the shared oracle\n\nThis entry moves the laws found in the earlier reviews into the shared\ninfinite-query suite where they are framework-independent, and leaves only\npre-commit work to the drivers.\n\n| Law | Owner |\n| --- | --- |\n| A synchronous source's first published value is ready with the first page. | Shared `first-paint-ready` |\n| Every ready value in a fixed-source, fixed-query scenario equals the source prefix for its own page count, with matching pages and continuation. | Shared, in 11 scenarios including `equal-dependency-depth` and `circular-dependency` |\n| A mount requests an on-demand first window once. | Shared `on-demand-paging` |\n| A render that never commits, or a superseded pre-commit recompute, does not acquire an on-demand source, direct or wrapped; commit does. Retired by the addendum \"On-demand sources follow useLiveQuery\"; replaced by acquisition parity with `useLiveQuery`. | React `infinite-query-render-cuts-oracle.test.tsx`, Svelte hook tests |\n| A StrictMode double render requests an on-demand first window once. Narrowed by the same addendum to React 19, where both hooks keep refs across the double render. | React render cuts |\n| After GC reclaims an abandoned render's collection, the retry's first commit is the ready first page. | React render cuts |\n\nEach shared handle now records every value its framework published, which\nreplaces the single first-paint recorder. Pre-commit work stays in drivers,\nbecause Vue's setup is its commit.\n\nEvidence, produced on the working tree above `0f0e05cbb`:\n\n- With a one-page replacement window, `equal-dependency-depth` and\n  `circular-dependency` fail in React and in Svelte. The Svelte half of the\n  retained-page regression was real; no earlier test could observe it.\n- With `startSync: false`, `first-paint-ready` fails in React and Svelte.\n- In the React render-cuts file, removing the nested walk fails only the\n  wrapped abandoned-render test. Starting every source in render also fails the\n  StrictMode test.\n- The four React-only files from earlier entries are merged into one. Their\n  retained-page test is dropped, because the shared law covers it in every\n  driver.\n- Local suites: react-db 335, vue-db 121, svelte-db 119, and the db infinite\n  calibration 9.\n\nA probe found one more open cell. A supplied collection that has not started\npublishes an idle first value in React and Svelte, and a ready one in Vue.\n`useLiveQuery` starts a supplied collection in render. The infinite hook does\nnot, and a React test requires that an abandoned render leave a supplied\ncollection unsubscribed. Closing this cell changes that contract, so it is\nrecorded for decision rather than changed here.\n\n## Addendum: supplied collections match useLiveQuery\n\nDecision: the maintainer chose to make `useLiveInfiniteQuery` behave like\n`useLiveQuery` for a supplied collection, and to reverse the rule from #1675\nthat no supplied collection starts before commit. That rule came from a #1675\nreview, which asked that collection construction be inert so that a render\nthat never commits leaves no resources behind. This PR keeps the purpose of\nthat rule with two safeguards: GC reclaims an abandoned start, and on-demand\nsources still wait for commit. The contract test\n`does not activate a supplied collection for an abandoned render` is replaced\nby `reclaims a supplied collection that an abandoned render started`.\n\nA supplied collection starts during render only when no source behind it is\non-demand and its window already holds the requested rows from offset 0. A\nshifted or narrower window waits for the controller to adjust it at commit.\nBoth hooks now validate a query or collection before starting it, so a\nrejected `.findOne()` query never reads its source.\n\nEvidence, produced on the working tree above `083247d18`:\n\n- The shared `first-paint-ready-collection` scenario failed in React and\n  Svelte with an idle first value, and passed in Vue, before the change. All\n  three drivers pass after it.\n- `collection-window-normalization` now checks every ready value. A supplied\n  gate that ignores the window fails it.\n- A supplied gate that ignores on-demand sources fails the supplied-input case\n  of the React render-cuts abandoned-render test.\n- `findone-runtime` now requires an unread source. Starting before validation\n  fails it with two subscribers.\n- `live-query-window-controller.test.ts` adds a controlled-premise witness for\n  a commit that arrives after the 50 ms GC floor. The collection is cleaned up\n  first, and subscribing restarts it without publishing a non-ready value. A\n  restart guard that skips cleaned-up collections fails it. Delivering the\n  subscribe handshake's publications to the listener does not. The witness\n  drives the controller directly, because a test DOM cannot hold a React\n  commit, so it does not prove React scheduling.\n- The walk from a live-query Collection to its sources is one shared helper,\n  `everySourceCollection`, which the observer's persisted-readiness check also\n  uses. The addendum \"Supplied windows request only what the hook needs\"\n  removes it with the on-demand gate it served.\n- Local suites: react-db 338, vue-db 122, svelte-db 120, and the full db suite\n  of 8267 tests.\n\nStill open: an on-demand source that loads synchronously publishes ready\nfirst in `useLiveQuery` and idle first in `useLiveInfiniteQuery`, because the\ninfinite hook defers on-demand sources to commit. The two hooks differ there\nuntil that choice is made. With a DbClient, a source that is first created\nduring render waits for commit in both hooks, by the deferral contract from\n#1564.\n\n## Addendum: on-demand sources follow useLiveQuery\n\nDecision: the maintainer chose that `useLiveInfiniteQuery` follow\n`useLiveQuery` for on-demand sources too, unless that is plainly wrong. The\ntwo rules conflict for an on-demand source. Ready on the first paint needs a\nrequest during render, and no request before commit forbids one.\n`useLiveQuery` starts on-demand sources in render, so the infinite hook now\ndoes too. The start gate is removed, and the law that a render that never\ncommits sends no on-demand request is retired. A parity law replaces it: under\nthe same history, the infinite hook acquires an on-demand source as often as\n`useLiveQuery` does, and commits the same first value.\n\nTwo parts of `useLiveQuery` were matched in substance, not copied:\n\n- `useLiveQuery` dedupes a React 19 StrictMode double render with a\n  render-time instance memo. The infinite hook now keeps the window collection\n  from its latest render and reuses it when the query identity matches, the\n  collection is not `cleaned-up`, and its window holds the retained pages. It\n  keeps only the collection. Each render still builds its own controller and\n  page count from committed state. One identity rule serves both the committed\n  and the rendered baseline.\n- A supplied collection starts in render as in `useLiveQuery`, but only when\n  its window already holds the requested rows from offset 0. Starting a\n  shifted or narrower window would publish the wrong rows first, which breaks\n  the ready-value law.\n\nEvidence, produced on the working tree above `e6a31f6eb`:\n\n- Before the change, an on-demand source that loads synchronously committed\n  ready first in `useLiveQuery` and idle first in the infinite hook. With the\n  gate removed alone, a StrictMode mount sent two first-window requests in the\n  infinite hook and one in `useLiveQuery`. With the memo, both send one, and\n  both send the same count for an abandoned render.\n- React `infinite-query-render-cuts-oracle.test.tsx` compares both hooks for an\n  abandoned render with a direct source, a wrapped source, and a supplied\n  collection, and for a StrictMode mount. Deferring query collections to\n  commit fails the abandoned-render and StrictMode cases. Deferring supplied\n  collections fails the supplied case. Removing the memo fails the StrictMode\n  case. Removing the memo's liveness check fails the mounted suspended-update\n  retry with a `cleaned-up` commit.\n- `useLiveInfiniteQuery.svelte.test.ts` compares settled acquisition counts\n  with Svelte's `useLiveQuery` after construction and a superseded recompute,\n  for direct and wrapped sources. Deferring query collections fails it and\n  Svelte `first-paint-ready`. The two hooks issue their requests at different\n  moments inside the same tick; only the settled count is compared.\n- Local suites: react-db 337, vue-db 122, svelte-db 120, and the full db suite\n  of 8267 tests.\n\nRemaining exposure, shared with `useLiveQuery` by this decision: a render that\nnever commits, including a Suspense render that throws, sends on-demand\nrequests. On React 18, StrictMode does not keep refs across the double render,\nso both hooks send two first-window requests there. Removing early on-demand\nrequests from both hooks needs a change in the live-query Collection itself.\n\n## Addendum: supplied windows request only what the hook needs\n\nAn external review of `c3341af0f` raised ten findings. This entry records the\nbehavioral ones.\n\nLaw: a mount requests no on-demand rows beyond the window the hook needs, for\neither input form. Authority: established behavior on `main`, where a supplied\ncollection waits for the controller to set its window at commit. The shared\n`on-demand-paging` scenario covered only query callbacks, and the supplied\nscenarios used an exact `.limit(4)` window, so a wider window was never\ngenerated. The new shared scenario `on-demand-collection-window` declares a\nwider `.limit(10)` and an exact `.limit(4)`, and checks the rows the\non-demand source holds after mount, which are the rows it was asked for.\n\n- With the earlier rule, which started any window at least as wide as needed,\n  the scenario fails in React and Svelte: the source loads all eight rows. It\n  passes on `main` and with the repair. Vue starts at commit and passes both.\n- The repair starts a supplied collection in render only when its window is\n  exactly the needed one, or unbounded. An unbounded window, from a query with\n  no `.limit`, sends one unbounded request on `main` too, because the\n  controller cannot narrow it before the source request, so starting it in\n  render adds no request and keeps its first paint ready. That pre-existing\n  unbounded request is outside this law and stays open.\n- The React render-time reuse path uses the same rule. A reuse mutant that\n  accepts a wider window survives every suite: the reused collection's rows\n  are already loaded, so no observation distinguishes it. No harm was found.\n- In Svelte, starting sync inside `$derived.by` can throw\n  `state_unsafe_mutation` when the start synchronously writes rows a peer's\n  pending load is waiting for. A two-component probe reproduces it for the\n  infinite hook and, on `main`, for Svelte's `useLiveQuery`, which starts in a\n  derived too. On the follow-up branch, where a live-query Collection defers\n  acquisition until a subscriber or preload, the same probe passes for both\n  hooks.\n  Decision: the maintainer accepted this exposure in this PR, shared with\n  `useLiveQuery`, and the deferred-acquisition follow-up removes it for both.\n- On React 18, StrictMode does not keep refs across the double render, so both\n  hooks still send two first-window requests. The changeset now names React 19.\n\n## Addendum: render-time reuse follows useLiveQuery's identity and error laws\n\nA second external review of `16a34e061` reproduced two React regressions in\nthe render-time collection cache, both against `useLiveQuery` as the\nreference.\n\n- A mounted replacement whose synchronous startup throws reached the error\n  boundary under `useLiveQuery` but committed `status: error` under the\n  infinite hook. React retried the render, and the retry reused the collection\n  the failed render had cached before it started. The hook now caches a created\n  collection only after it validated and started. The render-cut oracle,\n  renamed `infinite-query-render-cuts-oracle.test.tsx`, compares both hooks on\n  this history; caching before startup fails the infinite case.\n- A mounted hook rerendered with a different source object under a claimed\n  Collection ID showed the old source's rows, after a suspended update and,\n  before this PR, after a committed one too. `useLiveQuery` rejects that\n  history. Its rule now lives in `source-id-bindings.ts`, shared by both hooks,\n  and the source ID reuse oracle adds an infinite-hook driver over its model:\n  direct reuse, reuse after another ID, the same object and a new ID, reuse\n  after a suspended render, and release of a shared descriptor's sync deferral\n  on rejection. Removing the infinite hook's claim fails five of them, and a\n  claim that releases nothing fails the deferral case.\n- `on-demand-paging` and the exact supplied window now assert the first value\n  itself, not only later ready values, and the StrictMode parity test asserts\n  one first-window request on React 19. An infinite hook that leaves query\n  collections unstarted in render fails `on-demand-paging`, and one without\n  render-time reuse fails the StrictMode test.\n\nA third review of `f490f3d4d` found that the cache also reused a collection\nthat entered terminal error after it was cached, for example when its source\nrestarted after cleanup, so selecting its query again showed stale rows with\n`status: error`. `useLiveQuery` builds a fresh collection there. The cache now\nrejects a collection in terminal error. The render-cut oracle compares both\nhooks on that history and checks the restarted source's rows; dropping the\nerror check fails the infinite case. A synchronous load failure still throws\nfrom render in both hooks, and an asynchronous one settles after commit, so\nneither path reaches the cache.\n\nThe deferred-acquisition follow-up must revisit the new first-value assertion\nfor `on-demand-paging`: before a subscriber, its ordered on-demand window is\nunpublished, so that law and this one conflict and need a decision there.\n\n## Revision index\n\nEach entry's evidence applies to the revision named here. The first section's\ntable and closure apply to `415a8d4a1`.\n\n| Entry | Revision |\n| --- | --- |\n| Record and requirement outcomes | `415a8d4a1` (record added in `1c74a335d`) |\n| Duplicate pre-commit render fix | `a4af6a251` |\n| Cleaned-up reuse liveness check | `fc5f136a5` |\n| Start-sync gate replaces render-time reuse | `b31b84ff9` |\n| Nested on-demand sources and retained-page windows | `2b461f8cd` |\n| Publication laws in the shared oracle | `083247d18` |\n| Supplied collections match useLiveQuery | `e6a31f6eb` |\n| On-demand sources follow useLiveQuery | `c3341af0f` |\n| Supplied windows request only what the hook needs | `ff7549054` |\n| Render-time reuse follows useLiveQuery's identity and error laws | the commit that adds this entry, on top of `16a34e061` |\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# Infinite-query first-paint oracle\n\n- Reviewed semantic commit: `415a8d4a1ee0b950eca6f06f25c250adab05a15d`\n- Comparison main: `987f6ca0dfc404de8c76bd8c2b840421cc26311e`\n- Owner: `packages/db/tests/conformance/infinite-suite-oracle.ts`, scenario `[first-paint-ready]`\n- Supporting contract: `packages/db/tests/conformance/infinite-contract.ts` (`InfiniteQueryObservedHandle.firstPaint`, `mountObserved`)\n- Per-driver recorders: React `packages/react-db/tests/infinite-query-conformance.test.tsx`, Vue `packages/vue-db/tests/infinite-query-conformance.test.ts`, Svelte `packages/svelte-db/tests/infinite-query-conformance.svelte.test.ts`\n- Independent cross-hook reference: `packages/react-db/tests/infinite-query-first-commit-parity.test.tsx`\n- Runtime: local Node, Vitest. React ran in full. Vue and Svelte ran before the rebase and again under per-framework spot checks. CI runs every package suite.\n\n## New term\n\nThis change introduces the observation term **first paint**. A first paint is\nthe first value a framework binding publishes to its consumer after mount. The\nterm is not in the project glossary yet. Each driver records the first paint\nthrough its own native commit hook, because the frameworks reach it through\ndifferent cuts:\n\n- React records every layout commit and keeps the first. `current()` cannot\n  serve the law, because React can coalesce the idle commit before `renderHook`\n  returns.\n- Vue reads the hook result immediately after setup. Its `watchEffect` with\n  `flush: 'sync'` already subscribed and produced the first value.\n- Svelte reads the construction snapshot before `flushSync`, which is its first\n  render value, ahead of the subscribing `$effect`.\n\nThe term describes a cross-framework observation. It does not combine or split a\nproduction concept.\n\n## Requirement outcomes\n\n| Requirement | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 | Pass. The law: for a synchronously loaded source, `useLiveInfiniteQuery` shows the ready first page on the first paint, with no empty idle paint before data. Limit: synchronous eager sources and the query-callback input form. The recorder observes a framework commit value, not a browser paint. |\n| ORC-002 | Pass. The expected first page `['1','2','3']` comes from the source rows and the `orderBy(rank desc)` plus `pageSize` arguments, independent of the hook output. The parity test adds `useLiveQuery` as a documented prior-behavior reference. Neither reads the controller snapshot logic to compute the expectation. |\n| ORC-003 | Pass. The scenario prose states the law and why `current()` cannot observe it. The contract comment states the first-paint obligation and the per-framework cut. The driver records the real hook; the checkpoint reads `firstPaint()` after flush. |\n| ORC-004 | Not applicable. The trigger is a generated property that claims history coverage. This is a fixed example scenario over one synchronous source. It makes no generated-history coverage claim. |\n| ORC-005 | Pass. Each driver mounts the real framework hook and records the real first paint through the framework commit hook, then flushes to `ready`. The checkpoint is the first recorded paint. |\n| ORC-006 | Pass. Mutant calibration: reverting React or Svelte to `startSync: false` makes `[first-paint-ready]` fail at the first-paint checkpoint with `{ status: 'idle', ids: [] }`. This is an assertion failure at the intended checkpoint, not a setup error. The fixed `startSync: true` passes. |\n| ORC-007 | Not applicable. The trigger is an important generated property. This scenario has no random campaign. Direct replay is running the named `[first-paint-ready]` scenario in each driver package. |\n| ORC-008 | Not applicable. The change adds no production state and no stateful model. `firstPaint()` is a single test-only observation; it does not introduce, remove, combine, or split a modeled state. |\n| ORC-009 | Pass. The one new term is \"first paint\", declared above with its per-framework mapping. No production concept is combined or split. |\n| ORC-010 | Pass. The React recorder keeps the first observed commit and throws when no commit was observed, so it cannot silently report an empty paint. Cleanup uses the suite's existing `track()` and lifetime teardown; it does not replace the assertion. |\n| ORC-011 | Pass. The parity test is an independent second formulation: it asserts that `useLiveQuery` and `useLiveInfiniteQuery` agree on the first commit for the same synchronous source. No further distinct semantic classifier was identified. |\n| ORC-012 | Pass. This versioned record accounts for ORC-001 through ORC-014 at the reviewed semantic commit above. The pull-request description alone did not satisfy this requirement. |\n| ORC-013 | Pass. The scenario protects a reusable cross-framework boundary law. The distinguishing witness is the mutant: `startSync: false` fails for React and Svelte at the first-paint checkpoint, while the fix passes. Vue's distinct sync-subscribe cut stays green under both, which the law allows. |\n| ORC-014 | Not applicable. No controlled provider or host supplies a premise. The source is a standard synchronous mock collection shared with the rest of the suite. |\n\n## Bug-class closure\n\nThe claim is bounded, not universal.\n\n- Boundary. Contract: ready first page on the first paint for a synchronous\n  source. History: mount of a query-form `useLiveInfiniteQuery` over a\n  synchronous eager source, plus React dependency replacement. Production path:\n  the React, Vue, and Svelte hooks that build the live-query window collection.\n  Observation: the first paint status and first-page ids.\n- Distinguishing witnesses. Original: `startSync: false` yields an idle first\n  paint for React and Svelte. Adjacent: Vue's `watchEffect({ flush: 'sync' })`\n  yields a ready first paint even with `startSync: false`, which shows the law\n  constrains the observable first paint, not the flag.\n- Rejected wrong design. `startSync: false`, the production defect, is rejected\n  at the first-paint checkpoint for React and Svelte.\n\nUnresolved in-scope cells remain open under the owner above:\n\n- Pre-created collection input form, rather than the query callback.\n- Suspense, concurrent, or StrictMode first paint.\n\nOut-of-scope cells, where an empty or loading first paint is correct:\n\n- Asynchronous or on-demand sources that are not yet loaded.\n- `dbClient`-materialized sources whose sync is deferred to commit.\n\n## Verification\n\n- react-db: the full suite passed locally (330 tests), including this scenario\n  and the mutant run.\n- Vue and Svelte: `[first-paint-ready]` and the package suites passed under spot\n  checks. I could not re-run them against the latest `main` locally, because the\n  sandbox npm proxy returned 403 for unrelated security-bumped dependencies. CI\n  runs every package suite.\n- This record does not mark any CI check green.\n\n## Addendum: duplicate pre-commit render fix\n\nA follow-up review found a regression from `startSync: true`: because the hooks\nbuilt a new collection per render and only recorded it at commit, a duplicate\npre-commit render (React StrictMode, a discarded concurrent render, or a\nSuspense retry) started a second collection and loaded an on-demand source's\nfirst page twice. `useLiveQuery` avoids this with a render-time instance memo\n(its pool excludes window and on-demand queries, so the pool is not the cause).\n\nFix: React now records each render's state in a render-time ref and reuses it\nwhen every identity input matches, while committed state is still recorded at\nsubscribe so an abandoned render cannot overwrite preserved pages. Svelte's\n`$derived` controller now reuses the previous controller when nothing that\ndefines it changed, instead of rebuilding on every recompute.\n\nRegression: `packages/react-db/tests/infinite-query-strictmode-dedup.test.tsx`\nmounts an on-demand source under StrictMode and asserts the first peek-ahead\nwindow loads once. It fails (two loads) when the memo is recorded only at\ncommit, and passes with the render-time reuse. This closes the StrictMode part\nof the previously unresolved cell. The pre-created-collection input form and\nSuspense or concurrent first paint remain open under the owner above.\n\nA later review found that the React reuse could bind a collection that GC had\nalready cleaned up. A committed component keeps its refs across a suspended\nupdate, so the update's uncommitted collection survived to the retry, and the\nretry committed an empty `cleaned-up` page before recovering. The reuse now\nrequires a collection that is not `cleaned-up`.\n`packages/react-db/tests/infinite-query-stale-reuse.test.tsx` covers both a\nfresh-mount retry and a mounted suspended update after GC. The mounted-update\ncase fails without the liveness check and passes with it.\n\n## Addendum: start-sync gate replaces render-time reuse\n\nA further review found that the two reuse mechanisms above caused new\ndefects. In Svelte, the reuse guard returned the previous controller when the\n`deps` getters were unchanged, so the hook ignored reactive state read directly\ninside the query callback. In React, `renderedRef` could keep a stale page\ncount. It did not dedupe React 18 StrictMode renders, and it duplicated the\nidentity rules.\n\nBoth mechanisms are removed. The duplicate page requests came only from\non-demand sources, which load asynchronously and gain nothing from an early\nstart. `canStartLiveQueryWindowSyncInRender` now starts sync during render only\nwhen no source collection is on-demand. Eager sources keep the ready first\npaint. On-demand queries start when the subscription commits.\n\nEvidence at this change:\n\n- `infinite-query-strictmode-dedup.test.tsx` passes with one first-page load.\n  It fails with two loads when the gate always starts sync.\n- `useLiveInfiniteQuery.svelte.test.ts` adds a test where state read inside the\n  query callback, with no deps, rebuilds the query. It fails with the removed\n  Svelte guard and passes now.\n- `infinite-query-stale-reuse.test.tsx` passes. A retry after GC binds a live\n  collection and shows the ready first page.\n- Local suites passed: react-db 333, vue-db 121, svelte-db 118, and the db\n  infinite calibration 9.\n\nKnown limit: an eager collection started during render has only the 50 ms\nunsubscribed GC floor before its commit. If a commit arrives later than that,\nGC may clean the collection up first. `useLiveQuery` shares this exposure. No\ntest reaches this case.\n\n## Addendum: nested on-demand sources and retained-page windows\n\nA review of `725e37a28ac81e3d5041d990eb4abeebedc60399` reproduced two\nregressions from starting sync in render:\n\n- The start gate read only a query's immediate sources. A live-query\n  Collection does not copy its sources' sync mode, so an on-demand source\n  behind one passed the gate. An abandoned React Suspense render then sent a\n  page request. The gate now follows each live-query Collection to the sources\n  it reads.\n- A dependency replacement that keeps page depth built its collection with a\n  one-page window and started it in render. React committed a `ready` result\n  with fewer rows than the retained pages and `hasNextPage: false`, then\n  corrected it. React and Svelte now size the new window for every retained\n  page.\n\nEvidence, produced on the working tree above that commit:\n\n- `packages/react-db/tests/infinite-query-render-start.test.tsx` has three\n  tests. A wrapped on-demand source gets no acquisition from an abandoned\n  render, and a committed control acquires it. Every ready commit after an\n  equal dependency replacement keeps all retained rows and continuation.\n- Mutants: without the nested walk, only the abandoned-render test fails. With\n  a one-page window, only the retained-pages test fails.\n- `useLiveInfiniteQuery.svelte.test.ts` checks that no on-demand source, direct\n  or wrapped, is acquired at construction or by a superseded recompute before\n  the subscribing effect runs, and that one is acquired after it runs. Against\n  a `db` build without the nested walk, the wrapped case loaded once at\n  construction.\n- Local suites: react-db 336, vue-db 121, svelte-db 119, and the db infinite\n  calibration 9.\n\nSvelte's retained-page window was fixed by the same rule, but no test observes\nan intermediate Svelte value. The coverage map lists that cell.\n\n## Addendum: publication laws in the shared oracle\n\nThis entry moves the laws found in the earlier reviews into the shared\ninfinite-query suite where they are framework-independent, and leaves only\npre-commit work to the drivers.\n\n| Law | Owner |\n| --- | --- |\n| A synchronous source's first published value is ready with the first page. | Shared `first-paint-ready` |\n| Every ready value in a fixed-source, fixed-query scenario equals the source prefix for its own page count, with matching pages and continuation. | Shared, in 11 scenarios including `equal-dependency-depth` and `circular-dependency` |\n| A mount requests an on-demand first window once. | Shared `on-demand-paging` |\n| A render that never commits, or a superseded pre-commit recompute, does not acquire an on-demand source, direct or wrapped; commit does. Retired by the addendum \"On-demand sources follow useLiveQuery\"; replaced by acquisition parity with `useLiveQuery`. | React `infinite-query-render-cuts-oracle.test.tsx`, Svelte hook tests |\n| A StrictMode double render requests an on-demand first window once. Narrowed by the same addendum to React 19, where both hooks keep refs across the double render. | React render cuts |\n| After GC reclaims an abandoned render's collection, the retry's first commit is the ready first page. | React render cuts |\n\nEach shared handle now records every value its framework published, which\nreplaces the single first-paint recorder. Pre-commit work stays in drivers,\nbecause Vue's setup is its commit.\n\nEvidence, produced on the working tree above `0f0e05cbb`:\n\n- With a one-page replacement window, `equal-dependency-depth` and\n  `circular-dependency` fail in React and in Svelte. The Svelte half of the\n  retained-page regression was real; no earlier test could observe it.\n- With `startSync: false`, `first-paint-ready` fails in React and Svelte.\n- In the React render-cuts file, removing the nested walk fails only the\n  wrapped abandoned-render test. Starting every source in render also fails the\n  StrictMode test.\n- The four React-only files from earlier entries are merged into one. Their\n  retained-page test is dropped, because the shared law covers it in every\n  driver.\n- Local suites: react-db 335, vue-db 121, svelte-db 119, and the db infinite\n  calibration 9.\n\nA probe found one more open cell. A supplied collection that has not started\npublishes an idle first value in React and Svelte, and a ready one in Vue.\n`useLiveQuery` starts a supplied collection in render. The infinite hook does\nnot, and a React test requires that an abandoned render leave a supplied\ncollection unsubscribed. Closing this cell changes that contract, so it is\nrecorded for decision rather than changed here.\n\n## Addendum: supplied collections match useLiveQuery\n\nDecision: the maintainer chose to make `useLiveInfiniteQuery` behave like\n`useLiveQuery` for a supplied collection, and to reverse the rule from #1675\nthat no supplied collection starts before commit. That rule came from a #1675\nreview, which asked that collection construction be inert so that a render\nthat never commits leaves no resources behind. This PR keeps the purpose of\nthat rule with two safeguards: GC reclaims an abandoned start, and on-demand\nsources still wait for commit. The contract test\n`does not activate a supplied collection for an abandoned render` is replaced\nby `reclaims a supplied collection that an abandoned render started`.\n\nA supplied collection starts during render only when no source behind it is\non-demand and its window already holds the requested rows from offset 0. A\nshifted or narrower window waits for the controller to adjust it at commit.\nBoth hooks now validate a query or collection before starting it, so a\nrejected `.findOne()` query never reads its source.\n\nEvidence, produced on the working tree above `083247d18`:\n\n- The shared `first-paint-ready-collection` scenario failed in React and\n  Svelte with an idle first value, and passed in Vue, before the change. All\n  three drivers pass after it.\n- `collection-window-normalization` now checks every ready value. A supplied\n  gate that ignores the window fails it.\n- A supplied gate that ignores on-demand sources fails the supplied-input case\n  of the React render-cuts abandoned-render test.\n- `findone-runtime` now requires an unread source. Starting before validation\n  fails it with two subscribers.\n- `live-query-window-controller.test.ts` adds a controlled-premise witness for\n  a commit that arrives after the 50 ms GC floor. The collection is cleaned up\n  first, and subscribing restarts it without publishing a non-ready value. A\n  restart guard that skips cleaned-up collections fails it. Delivering the\n  subscribe handshake's publications to the listener does not. The witness\n  drives the controller directly, because a test DOM cannot hold a React\n  commit, so it does not prove React scheduling.\n- The walk from a live-query Collection to its sources is one shared helper,\n  `everySourceCollection`, which the observer's persisted-readiness check also\n  uses. The addendum \"Supplied windows request only what the hook needs\"\n  removes it with the on-demand gate it served.\n- Local suites: react-db 338, vue-db 122, svelte-db 120, and the full db suite\n  of 8267 tests.\n\nStill open: an on-demand source that loads synchronously publishes ready\nfirst in `useLiveQuery` and idle first in `useLiveInfiniteQuery`, because the\ninfinite hook defers on-demand sources to commit. The two hooks differ there\nuntil that choice is made. With a DbClient, a source that is first created\nduring render waits for commit in both hooks, by the deferral contract from\n#1564.\n\n## Addendum: on-demand sources follow useLiveQuery\n\nDecision: the maintainer chose that `useLiveInfiniteQuery` follow\n`useLiveQuery` for on-demand sources too, unless that is plainly wrong. The\ntwo rules conflict for an on-demand source. Ready on the first paint needs a\nrequest during render, and no request before commit forbids one.\n`useLiveQuery` starts on-demand sources in render, so the infinite hook now\ndoes too. The start gate is removed, and the law that a render that never\ncommits sends no on-demand request is retired. A parity law replaces it: under\nthe same history, the infinite hook acquires an on-demand source as often as\n`useLiveQuery` does, and commits the same first value.\n\nTwo parts of `useLiveQuery` were matched in substance, not copied:\n\n- `useLiveQuery` dedupes a React 19 StrictMode double render with a\n  render-time instance memo. The infinite hook now keeps the window collection\n  from its latest render and reuses it when the query identity matches, the\n  collection is not `cleaned-up`, and its window holds the retained pages. It\n  keeps only the collection. Each render still builds its own controller and\n  page count from committed state. One identity rule serves both the committed\n  and the rendered baseline.\n- A supplied collection starts in render as in `useLiveQuery`, but only when\n  its window already holds the requested rows from offset 0. Starting a\n  shifted or narrower window would publish the wrong rows first, which breaks\n  the ready-value law.\n\nEvidence, produced on the working tree above `e6a31f6eb`:\n\n- Before the change, an on-demand source that loads synchronously committed\n  ready first in `useLiveQuery` and idle first in the infinite hook. With the\n  gate removed alone, a StrictMode mount sent two first-window requests in the\n  infinite hook and one in `useLiveQuery`. With the memo, both send one, and\n  both send the same count for an abandoned render.\n- React `infinite-query-render-cuts-oracle.test.tsx` compares both hooks for an\n  abandoned render with a direct source, a wrapped source, and a supplied\n  collection, and for a StrictMode mount. Deferring query collections to\n  commit fails the abandoned-render and StrictMode cases. Deferring supplied\n  collections fails the supplied case. Removing the memo fails the StrictMode\n  case. Removing the memo's liveness check fails the mounted suspended-update\n  retry with a `cleaned-up` commit.\n- `useLiveInfiniteQuery.svelte.test.ts` compares settled acquisition counts\n  with Svelte's `useLiveQuery` after construction and a superseded recompute,\n  for direct and wrapped sources. Deferring query collections fails it and\n  Svelte `first-paint-ready`. The two hooks issue their requests at different\n  moments inside the same tick; only the settled count is compared.\n- Local suites: react-db 337, vue-db 122, svelte-db 120, and the full db suite\n  of 8267 tests.\n\nRemaining exposure, shared with `useLiveQuery` by this decision: a render that\nnever commits, including a Suspense render that throws, sends on-demand\nrequests. On React 18, StrictMode does not keep refs across the double render,\nso both hooks send two first-window requests there. Removing early on-demand\nrequests from both hooks needs a change in the live-query Collection itself.\n\n## Addendum: supplied windows request only what the hook needs\n\nAn external review of `c3341af0f` raised ten findings. This entry records the\nbehavioral ones.\n\nLaw: a mount requests no on-demand rows beyond the window the hook needs, for\neither input form. Authority: established behavior on `main`, where a supplied\ncollection waits for the controller to set its window at commit. The shared\n`on-demand-paging` scenario covered only query callbacks, and the supplied\nscenarios used an exact `.limit(4)` window, so a wider window was never\ngenerated. The new shared scenario `on-demand-collection-window` declares a\nwider `.limit(10)` and an exact `.limit(4)`, and checks the rows the\non-demand source holds after mount, which are the rows it was asked for.\n\n- With the earlier rule, which started any window at least as wide as needed,\n  the scenario fails in React and Svelte: the source loads all eight rows. It\n  passes on `main` and with the repair. Vue starts at commit and passes both.\n- The repair starts a supplied collection in render only when its window is\n  exactly the needed one, or unbounded. An unbounded window, from a query with\n  no `.limit`, sends one unbounded request on `main` too, because the\n  controller cannot narrow it before the source request, so starting it in\n  render adds no request and keeps its first paint ready. That pre-existing\n  unbounded request is outside this law and stays open.\n- The React render-time reuse path uses the same rule. A reuse mutant that\n  accepts a wider window survives every suite: the reused collection's rows\n  are already loaded, so no observation distinguishes it. No harm was found.\n- In Svelte, starting sync inside `$derived.by` can throw\n  `state_unsafe_mutation` when the start synchronously writes rows a peer's\n  pending load is waiting for. A two-component probe reproduces it for the\n  infinite hook and, on `main`, for Svelte's `useLiveQuery`, which starts in a\n  derived too. On the follow-up branch, where a live-query Collection defers\n  acquisition until a subscriber or preload, the same probe passes for both\n  hooks.\n  Decision: the maintainer accepted this exposure in this PR, shared with\n  `useLiveQuery`, and the deferred-acquisition follow-up removes it for both.\n- On React 18, StrictMode does not keep refs across the double render, so both\n  hooks still send two first-window requests. The changeset now names React 19.\n\n## Addendum: render-time reuse follows useLiveQuery's identity and error laws\n\nA second external review of `16a34e061` reproduced two React regressions in\nthe render-time collection cache, both against `useLiveQuery` as the\nreference.\n\n- A mounted replacement whose synchronous startup throws reached the error\n  boundary under `useLiveQuery` but committed `status: error` under the\n  infinite hook. React retried the render, and the retry reused the collection\n  the failed render had cached before it started. The hook now caches a created\n  collection only after it validated and started. The render-cut oracle,\n  renamed `infinite-query-render-cuts-oracle.test.tsx`, compares both hooks on\n  this history; caching before startup fails the infinite case.\n- A mounted hook rerendered with a different source object under a claimed\n  Collection ID showed the old source's rows, after a suspended update and,\n  before this PR, after a committed one too. `useLiveQuery` rejects that\n  history. Its rule now lives in `source-id-bindings.ts`, shared by both hooks,\n  and the source ID reuse oracle adds an infinite-hook driver over its model:\n  direct reuse, reuse after another ID, the same object and a new ID, reuse\n  after a suspended render, and release of a shared descriptor's sync deferral\n  on rejection. Removing the infinite hook's claim fails five of them, and a\n  claim that releases nothing fails the deferral case.\n- `on-demand-paging` and the exact supplied window now assert the first value\n  itself, not only later ready values, and the StrictMode parity test asserts\n  one first-window request on React 19. An infinite hook that leaves query\n  collections unstarted in render fails `on-demand-paging`, and one without\n  render-time reuse fails the StrictMode test.\n\nA third review of `f490f3d4d` found that the cache also reused a collection\nthat entered terminal error after it was cached, for example when its source\nrestarted after cleanup, so selecting its query again showed stale rows with\n`status: error`. `useLiveQuery` builds a fresh collection there. The cache now\nrejects a collection in terminal error. The render-cut oracle compares both\nhooks on that history and checks the restarted source's rows; dropping the\nerror check fails the infinite case. A synchronous load failure still throws\nfrom render in both hooks, and an asynchronous one settles after commit, so\nneither path reaches the cache.\n\nThe deferred-acquisition follow-up must revisit the new first-value assertion\nfor `on-demand-paging`: before a subscriber, its ordered on-demand window is\nunpublished, so that law and this one conflict and need a decision there.\n\n## Revision index\n\nEach entry's evidence applies to the revision named here. The first section's\ntable and closure apply to `415a8d4a1`.\n\n| Entry | Revision |\n| --- | --- |\n| Record and requirement outcomes | `415a8d4a1` (record added in `1c74a335d`) |\n| Duplicate pre-commit render fix | `a4af6a251` |\n| Cleaned-up reuse liveness check | `fc5f136a5` |\n| Start-sync gate replaces render-time reuse | `b31b84ff9` |\n| Nested on-demand sources and retained-page windows | `2b461f8cd` |\n| Publication laws in the shared oracle | `083247d18` |\n| Supplied collections match useLiveQuery | `e6a31f6eb` |\n| On-demand sources follow useLiveQuery | `c3341af0f` |\n| Supplied windows request only what the hook needs | `ff7549054` |\n| Render-time reuse follows useLiveQuery's identity and error laws | the commit that adds this entry, on top of `16a34e061` |\n"}
